.TH auto-pxe-node-discover 8

\" auto-man2man (used in generating subcommand lists) depends on this section
.SH NAME
.PP
auto-pxe-node-discover - Watch for and configure new nodes

\" Convention:
\" Underline anything that is typed verbatim - commands, etc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf 
.na
auto-pxe-node-discover basename digits domain
.ad
.fi

.SH ARGUMENTS
.nf
.na
basename    Base name for new nodes, e.g. "compute-", or "raid-"
digits      Number of digits in the serial # following basename
domain      Domain name to complete the FQDN
.ad
.fi

.SH DESCRIPTION

The
.B auto-pxe-node-discover
is used to configure new nodes on a local network, on a server
which is running dhcpd.
When a new node appears,
.B auto-pxe-node-discover
assigns it a hostname based on the command-line arguments as follows:

${basename}${serial_number}.${domain}

The serial_number is an integer containing exactly the number
of digits indicated on the command-line, so zero-prefixed, e.g.

compute-012.mycluster.edu, raid-04.mycluster.edu

.B auto-pxe-node-discover
then adds an entry to /etc/hosts, and disables the warning about
an unknown host key upon the first connection in /etc/ssh_config.

Finally, an entry is added to dhcp.conf to reserve the IP address.
This step is critical to maintain access to the server.

This tool is intended for configuring new cluster nodes, and
is used by higher-level tools such as SPCM (Simple Portable
Cluster Manager).

.SH RETURN VALUES

0 on success, non-zero error codes otherwise.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
.na
# To configure new compute nodes, maximum 999 nodes:
auto-pxe-node-discover compute- 3 mycluster.edu

# To configure new RAID servers, maximum 99 servers:
auto-pxe-node-discover raid- 2 mycluster.edu
.ad
.fi

.SH FILES
.nf
.na
/etc/hosts, /etc/ssh/ssh_config, dhcpd.conf
.ad
.fi

.SH SEE ALSO

auto-admin(8), auto-pxe-installer-setup(8)

